WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.804
Eduardo maintains the search over a health insurer's internal manual.

00:00:06.104 --> 00:00:11.192
Somebody cut the documents every five hundred tokens, ignoring the structure.

00:00:11.492 --> 00:00:13.652
And one of the chunks says only this.

00:00:14.302 --> 00:00:19.211
The deadline is twenty one business days from the request, and it may be extended

00:00:19.211 --> 00:00:20.726
once, with justification.

00:00:21.026 --> 00:00:24.023
In the cases covered by item three point two.

00:00:24.673 --> 00:00:26.702
Deadline for what?

00:00:27.002 --> 00:00:27.604
For whom?

00:00:27.904 --> 00:00:29.628
From which version of the manual?

00:00:29.928 --> 00:00:33.904
Search finds this passage for the authorization deadline for surgery.

00:00:34.204 --> 00:00:39.574
But also for the reimbursement deadline, because the passage never says which topic

00:00:39.574 --> 00:00:40.221
it covers.

00:00:40.521 --> 00:00:45.347
And the model answers with confidence, and sometimes gets the topic wrong.

00:00:45.997 --> 00:00:51.075
In this lesson, you'll learn to cut documents so that each chunk can be understood,

00:00:51.075 --> 00:00:52.421
and found, on its own.

00:00:53.621 --> 00:00:54.881
Think of a file card.

00:00:55.181 --> 00:00:57.563
A good one doesn't just say what it contains.

00:00:57.863 --> 00:01:01.817
It says which folder, which drawer, and which year it came from.

00:01:02.117 --> 00:01:05.213
The pattern makes two changes, both at indexing time.

00:01:05.863 --> 00:01:07.651
First, split by structure.

00:01:07.951 --> 00:01:12.577
Headings, sections, clauses, list items and tables are the boundaries.

00:01:12.877 --> 00:01:17.646
A chunk never starts mid sentence, and never separates a table from its header.

00:01:17.946 --> 00:01:21.539
There's still a maximum size, but it's the limit, not the rule.

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:24.628
Second, attach context.

00:01:24.928 --> 00:01:29.923
Each chunk gets a header with its path, document, chapter and section, its version,

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:34.437
and optionally one sentence, written by a model, that situates the passage.

00:01:35.087 --> 00:01:38.725
That header goes into the text you index, for the search.

00:01:39.025 --> 00:01:43.454
And into the text you send to the model, for the answer and the citation.

00:01:44.654 --> 00:01:49.182
The document arrives, as a PDF, a web page or plain text.

00:01:49.832 --> 00:01:53.525
A parser finds the headings, sections, lists and tables.

00:01:54.175 --> 00:01:57.901
The split follows that structure, within the maximum size.

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:03.503
A small model reads the document and the passage, and writes one sentence that says

00:02:03.503 --> 00:02:04.517
where it belongs.

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:10.700
Each chunk becomes a header, with path, version and that sentence, plus the original

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:11.029
text.

00:02:11.679 --> 00:02:16.100
And the full chunk is indexed, for keyword search and for vector search.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.832
Now the same passage, after the pattern.

00:02:20.132 --> 00:02:23.016
Three things changed, and each one teaches something.

00:02:23.666 --> 00:02:24.272
The path.

00:02:24.572 --> 00:02:30.286
Authorizations, then elective surgery, now goes into the embedding and into keyword

00:02:30.286 --> 00:02:30.768
search.

00:02:31.068 --> 00:02:34.602
A reimbursement question no longer looks just as similar.

00:02:35.252 --> 00:02:36.471
The context sentence.

00:02:36.771 --> 00:02:41.419
It translates the vague passage into the vocabulary of the question.

00:02:41.719 --> 00:02:45.255
Authorization, elective surgery, individual plan.

00:02:45.905 --> 00:02:47.353
And the neighboring clause.

00:02:47.653 --> 00:02:51.651
If the section fits the limit, item three point two travels with it.

00:02:51.951 --> 00:02:56.751
If it doesn't, the header still points at section four point three, and the citation

00:02:56.751 --> 00:02:59.037
doesn't pretend the passage is complete.

00:02:59.687 --> 00:03:00.842
What does it cost?

00:03:01.142 --> 00:03:04.456
Say eight hundred tokens of body and eighty of header.

00:03:04.756 --> 00:03:10.001
Five chunks per question go from four thousand tokens to four thousand four hundred.

00:03:10.301 --> 00:03:15.752
The real cost sits in ingestion, one small call per chunk, with the whole document

00:03:15.752 --> 00:03:16.217
cached.

00:03:16.517 --> 00:03:17.856
Not in the question.

00:03:18.506 --> 00:03:21.324
And putting the path at the start helps twice.

00:03:21.624 --> 00:03:23.224
The search finds the right topic.

00:03:23.524 --> 00:03:28.822
And the model, which reads the start of a context better than the middle, sees first

00:03:28.822 --> 00:03:30.588
where the passage came from.

00:03:31.788 --> 00:03:32.878
Here's one for you.

00:03:33.178 --> 00:03:38.322
You turned on the context sentence, but you generated it by reading only the chunk,

00:03:38.322 --> 00:03:39.376
not the document.

00:03:39.676 --> 00:03:45.468
In the eval, the reimbursement question still retrieves the authorization passage.

00:03:45.768 --> 00:03:46.270
Why?

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.159
Because the sentence is as vague as the chunk.

00:03:52.459 --> 00:03:55.311
The model writing it never saw the chapter either.

00:03:55.611 --> 00:04:00.014
So it writes something like, this passage discusses a deadline.

00:04:00.664 --> 00:04:04.717
The sentence exists to inject the context the chunk lost.

00:04:05.017 --> 00:04:08.742
Without the document in that call, there's no context to inject.

00:04:09.042 --> 00:04:14.161
That's why the call carries the whole document, cached, and reuses it across every

00:04:14.161 --> 00:04:14.535
chunk.

00:04:15.735 --> 00:04:18.549
Short documents that fit in a single chunk.

00:04:18.849 --> 00:04:22.168
A two page remote work policy doesn't need to be cut.

00:04:22.468 --> 00:04:25.587
A title and a version at the start are enough.

00:04:25.887 --> 00:04:29.909
Splitting it only invents boundaries, and can cut the rule in half.

00:04:30.559 --> 00:04:34.052
Unstructured content, like conversation transcripts.

00:04:34.352 --> 00:04:36.112
There are no headings to follow.

00:04:36.412 --> 00:04:39.809
Split by topic, or by overlapping windows.

00:04:40.459 --> 00:04:43.558
And material that fits whole in the context.

00:04:43.858 --> 00:04:46.119
That's the smell from two lessons ago.

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:48.691
Don't chunk what the model could read.

00:04:49.341 --> 00:04:50.932
Now the common traps.

00:04:51.232 --> 00:04:54.106
Cutting by characters and calling it structural.

00:04:54.406 --> 00:04:59.164
If the boundary ignores the table title, you only changed the size.

00:04:59.814 --> 00:05:02.706
Which is exactly what happened at the insurer.

00:05:03.006 --> 00:05:06.212
The rows with the deadline numbers landed in one chunk.

00:05:06.512 --> 00:05:12.345
The header, with the columns, authorization, reimbursement, appeal, stayed in the

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:13.281
previous one.

00:05:13.581 --> 00:05:18.020
So the bot started citing twenty one, without saying which deadline.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:22.417
A table becomes its own chunk, with its title and its columns.

00:05:23.067 --> 00:05:26.046
And leaving repealed versions in the index.

00:05:26.346 --> 00:05:31.523
A header that says version six doesn't stop the search from returning version six.

00:05:31.823 --> 00:05:36.591
Filter by effective date at query time, and remove the old document when you

00:05:36.591 --> 00:05:37.093
reindex.

00:05:37.393 --> 00:05:40.365
That's solved in the index, not in the prompt.

00:05:41.565 --> 00:05:42.594
What to measure.

00:05:42.894 --> 00:05:48.361
Search recall and precision, before and after, on the same eval.

00:05:49.011 --> 00:05:51.738
Build twin questions on purpose.

00:05:52.038 --> 00:05:55.726
The reimbursement deadline and the authorization deadline.

00:05:56.026 --> 00:06:00.449
If the reimbursement passage stays out of the top five for the authorization

00:06:00.449 --> 00:06:02.660
question, the header is doing its job.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:04.660
Check the citations.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:08.272
The section cited in the answer has to be the right one.

00:06:08.922 --> 00:06:12.454
And alert on the empty-path rate at ingestion.

00:06:12.754 --> 00:06:17.795
If thirty percent of the chunks come out with no path, the parser broke on a new

00:06:17.795 --> 00:06:18.551
kind of PDF.

00:06:18.851 --> 00:06:21.644
Don't paper over it with the generated sentence.

00:06:21.944 --> 00:06:24.523
Fix the conversion, and reindex.

00:06:25.173 --> 00:06:26.491
One security point.

00:06:26.791 --> 00:06:30.766
A chunk can carry personal data, a member's name, a card number.

00:06:31.066 --> 00:06:36.423
Under data protection law, the index is one more place where that data lives.

00:06:36.723 --> 00:06:41.652
If your policy forbids sending those documents to the model that writes the context

00:06:41.652 --> 00:06:46.106
sentence, stay on the structural header, built only from title and section.

00:06:47.306 --> 00:06:49.475
On the page, there are two tabs.

00:06:49.775 --> 00:06:52.640
The TypeScript one does only the structural part.

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:58.192
It splits the document by its headings, keeps the path as it goes down, and puts

00:06:58.192 --> 00:07:01.410
title, path and version at the top of each chunk.

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:03.408
No model call at all.

00:07:04.058 --> 00:07:06.847
The Python one adds the context sentence.

00:07:07.147 --> 00:07:12.245
It sends the whole document as a cached block, and asks a small model, for each

00:07:12.245 --> 00:07:15.342
chunk, which topic and case that passage covers.

00:07:15.992 --> 00:07:17.974
And that's the order to adopt it.

00:07:18.274 --> 00:07:20.036
Start with the structural header.

00:07:20.336 --> 00:07:23.804
It's cheap, and it already solves much of the problem.

00:07:24.454 --> 00:07:29.841
Turn on the generated sentence only if the topic confusion eval still fails.

00:07:31.041 --> 00:07:33.244
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:33.544 --> 00:07:36.635
A chunk cut blindly is a sentence without an address.

00:07:36.935 --> 00:07:42.238
Cut along the document's own structure, and give each piece its path, its version

00:07:42.238 --> 00:07:45.969
and, when it helps, a sentence that says what it's about.

00:07:46.269 --> 00:07:49.667
Then both the search and the model know where they are.

00:07:50.317 --> 00:07:55.907
That path in the header is made of exact words, and exact words are what keyword

00:07:55.907 --> 00:07:56.815
search loves.

00:07:57.115 --> 00:08:01.945
In the next lesson, we'll combine keyword search with embeddings: hybrid search.

00:08:02.245 --> 00:08:03.262
See you there.
